BUILD_ONLY: ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:robodojo-<images/robodojo/VERSION> is never public (docs/protocol/README.md §2.4,
D5). It is kept in the organization's private package as the build cache of the RoboDojo tasks: every task's Dockerfile
starts with this recipe (images/robodojo/Dockerfile, copied in by scripts/task_conventions.py normalize) and its compose
names the private image in `build.cache_from`. A runner that can pull the image takes its layers; any other runner builds
the same recipe from public sources on its first task (`harbor run` does it; nothing to run by hand).

Why
  The image contains NVIDIA Isaac Sim 5.1 / Omniverse Kit and the pip wheels of the Isaac stack (from pypi.nvidia.com).
  NVIDIA licenses them for use, not for redistribution, so the built image may not be published. Everything else in it may
  be: RoboDojo (github.com/RoboDojo-Benchmark/RoboDojo @ cb2a37d, MIT), its Isaac Lab fork (github.com/yuechen0614/IsaacLab
  @ afca7b0, BSD-3-Clause), its cuRobo fork (github.com/yuechen0614/curobo @ d17b54c, NVIDIA's cuRobo licence), XPolicyLab
  (@ 53c5da5, Apache-2.0; only its client code is imported, and RoboDojo's evaluator is used without a policy server), and
  RoboDojo's assets (Hugging Face dataset RoboDojo-Benchmark/RoboDojo @ 35efbc7, Assets/, Apache-2.0).

What the builder accepts
  - the NVIDIA Isaac Sim / Omniverse license agreement (the recipe sets OMNI_KIT_ACCEPT_EULA=YES and ACCEPT_EULA=Y, and
    every container that runs it accepts it again at launch);
  - NVIDIA's terms for the isaacsim wheels on pypi.nvidia.com and for cuRobo;
  - the licences above (MIT, BSD-3-Clause, Apache-2.0).
  No account, key or token is needed: everything is downloaded anonymously.

Inputs (all pinned)
  docker.io/nvidia/cuda:12.8.1-cudnn-devel-ubuntu22.04 by digest (cuRobo builds CUDA extensions); Ubuntu packages from
  snapshot.ubuntu.com at 2026-10-01; uv 0.13.0 (its image by digest) and the CPython 3.11.17 it installs; RoboDojo and
  its three submodules at the commits above; the assets from the dataset's git LFS at revision 35efbc7 (batched LFS
  requests: Hugging Face rate-limits anonymous per-file API downloads; every one of the 15365 files must arrive);
  RoboDojo's install.sh steps, after which every Python package is set to images/robodojo/requirements.lock (the
  reference build's `uv pip freeze`) without resolving, and the build fails unless the environment is exactly the lock;
  the Isaac Lab extension RoboDojo's fork pins (isaacsim.asset.importer.urdf 2.4.31), fetched from NVIDIA's extension
  registry by one start of the app; three MDL materials two asset USDs load from NVIDIA's asset bucket
  (images/robodojo/web_assets.txt, each checked against its sha256), baked in under /opt/isaac-assets with the USDs
  pointed at the copies (localize_assets.py). The recipe's three repository files come by URL from this repository at a
  pinned commit that a tag keeps reachable, each checked by its sha256: a change to one of them is a new commit and
  checksum in the recipe and a new VERSION.

Data
  RoboDojo's assets (Robots, Object, Material, Room, Background, Sensor, Traj, Eval_Layout: 15365 files, ~41 GB) are baked
  in under /opt/RoboDojo/Assets; the robots' cuRobo configs are generated from their templates as RoboDojo's install does.
  A task container has no general network access and Isaac Sim starts and renders offline. RoboDojo's training
  demonstrations (data/) and policy checkpoints (ckpt/) are NOT in the image. The frozen task instances (RoboDojo's own
  evaluation layouts) live in the task directories.

Host
  Linux x86_64, Docker with BuildKit and the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, an RTX-class NVIDIA GPU with a driver Isaac Sim
  5.1 supports (Isaac renders with RTX even headless, and RoboDojo runs PhysX with GPU dynamics). Checked on an RTX 4090
  (24 GB). Build: ~150 GB of free disk during the build (the image is ~75 GB), network access to github.com, ghcr.io,
  snapshot.ubuntu.com, pypi.org, download.pytorch.org, pypi.nvidia.com, huggingface.co (+ its LFS / Xet storage),
  NVIDIA's extension registry (ovextensionsprod.blob.core.windows.net, dw290v42wisod.cloudfront.net) and
  omniverse-content-production.s3*.amazonaws.com; a cold build takes 30-90 min (32 min on a 32-core host), most of it
  the 41 GB download. Run: ~5-10 GB of GPU memory and up to ~16 GB of memory per Isaac simulator
  (scripts/task_resources.toml). Isaac does not exit when the GPU runs out of memory (it waits on a crash dialog): on a
  shared GPU start fewer at once; an agent's own sessions, the standard sidecar and the verifier's replays each need
  one.

Build and publish (maintainers; from the repository root)
  RCB_PUSH_PRIVATE=1 scripts/build_images.sh builds every image (this one with BuildKit's inline cache); alone:
    docker build --build-arg BUILDKIT_INLINE_CACHE=1 -t ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:robodojo-$(cat images/robodojo/VERSION) images/robodojo
    docker push ghcr.io/mll-lab-nu/efe-private:robodojo-$(cat images/robodojo/VERSION)
  A new VERSION: change the tag in the tasks' `cache_from` and normalize them.

Check a build
  Every frozen instance must rebuild to its recorded state hash: `harbor run -p tasks/robodojo/robodojo-stack-bowls-i00-privileged
  -a oracle` (reward 1; the verifier's replay checks "instance_exact", except for the four tasks graded by verdict, whose
  resets are not bit-identical: instance.json says "determinism": "verdict").
